    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72759",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/189aa1ea4e33d47bd26027af4990020c837211a58d53668e25f9210353faa784.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
